{"qid":"Q389688","names":["Achaemenid Empire"],"title":"Achaemenid Empire","endonym":{"native":"𐎧𐏁𐏂","lang":"peo"},"capsule":"The Achaemenid Empire, founded by Cyrus II (the Great) who overthrew the Median kingdom in 550 BC and captured Babylon in 539 BC, became the largest empire the ancient world had yet seen, stretching from Thrace and Egypt to the Indus Valley under Darius I (r. 522–486 BC). Darius organized the empire into some twenty provinces (satrapies), constructed the Royal Road from Sardis to Susa, and enforced Aramaic as a bureaucratic lingua franca; his Behistun inscription provides the most detailed royal self-account from the ancient Near East. The empire survived Xerxes I's failed invasions of Greece (480–479 BC) but weakened through dynastic conflict until Alexander of Macedon defeated Darius III at Gaugamela in 331 BC and occupied Persepolis, ending Achaemenid rule; our dataset closes c. 327 BC as Alexander consolidated the eastern satrapies.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Core satrapal boundaries are reconstructed from the Persepolis Fortification Tablets, royal inscriptions (Behistun, Naqsh-e Rostam), and Herodotus's satrapy list; frontier zones in Central Asia and the Indus are attested by fewer administrative texts and rely substantially on numismatic and archaeological data, yielding moderate boundary certainty.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Achaemenid Empire","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Achaemenian-dynasty"},{"label":"Livius.org: Achaemenids","url":"https://www.livius.org/articles/dynasty/achaemenids/"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Achaemenid Empire","url"